```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- Powers of three are always positive, so return `false` for `n <= 0`.
- Keep dividing `n` by 3 while it's divisible.
- If the final result is 1, then `n` was a power of three.

**Follow-up (no loops/recursion):**
```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // 3^19 = 1162261463 is the largest power of 3 fitting in a 32-bit int
        return n > 0 && 1162261463 % n == 0;
    }
}
```
This works because any power of three must divide the largest power of three evenly.

